**Rust Refactored Function:**

```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs::create_dir_all;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

pub fn create_directory_with_env(dirname: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error> {
    let dir_path = env::var("DIR_PATH")?;
    if !Path::new(&dir_path).exists() {
        create_dir_all(&PathBuf::from(dir_path))?;
    }
    let mut path = Path::new(&dir_path);
    path.push(dirname);
    if !path.exists() {
        create_dir_all(&path)?;
    }
    env::set_var("DIR_PATH", &PathBuf::from(path).to_str().unwrap());
    Ok(())
}
```

This Rust function checks if the environment variable "DIR_PATH" is set, and if not, creates a new directory with the given name. If the variable is already set, it uses the existing value for the new directory path. The performance and memory usage of this refactored function should be optimal. However, you may consider using Rust's concurrency libraries to speed up the process when creating multiple directories or optimizing error handling for better resource utilization.']

