[' Below is a high-level design and code snippets for the Java service. I'll use Micronaut as the framework, Redis for caching, and Hystrix for circuit breakers.

```java
import io.micronaut.core.annotation.Introspected;
import io.micronaut.http.annotation.*;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import redis.clients.jedis.Jedis;
import software.amazon.awssdk.regions.Region;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.dynamodb.DynamoDbClient;
import com.netflix.hystrix.HystrixCommand;
import java.time.Instant;

@Introspected
@Controller("/payments")
public class PaymentService {

    private final DynamoDbClient dbClient;
    private final Jedis redisClient;

    public PaymentService(DynamoDbClient dbClient, Jedis redisClient) {
        this.dbClient = dbClient;
        this.redisClient = redisClient;
    }

    @Post("/{operationType}/{transactionId}")
    public ResponseEntity<?> execute(@Body JsonNode payload, @PathVariable String operationType, @PathVariable String transactionId) {
        // Validate JSON schema for CREATE and CAPTURE operations
        if (!validateSchema(payload)) {
            return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("Invalid JSON payload");
        }

        PaymentTransaction transaction = new PaymentTransaction(operationType, transactionId, payload);

        switch (operationType) {
            case "CREATE":
                // Save the payment details in DynamoDB
                saveToDynamoDb(transaction);
                break;
            case "CAPTURE":
                // Use Redis for caching and access validation
                String cacheKey = buildCacheKey(transactionId);
                transaction.setAccessCount(redisClient.incrBy(cacheKey, 1));
                if (transaction.getAccessCount() > 1) {
                    // Cache hit, process the transaction
                    handleTransaction(transaction);
                } else {
                    // Cache miss, fetch and store the transaction details from DynamoDB before processing
                    fetchFromDynamoDbAndHandle(transaction);
                }
                break;
            case "REFUND":
                // Check for eligibility and integrity using a hash and distributed ledger
                if (isEligibleForRefund(transaction)) {
                    handleTransaction(transaction);
                } else {
                    return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("Invalid transaction for refund");
                }
                break;
        }

        // Use HystrixCommand for circuit breaking and retrying transient failures
        HystrixCommand<Void> saveToDynamoDbCommand = HystrixCommand.Factory.create(KeyGenerator.generateKey(), () -> saveToDynamoDb(transaction));
        saveToDynamoDbCommand.execute();

        return ResponseEntity.ok().body("Transaction processed successfully");
    }

    // Implement other methods like validateSchema, buildCacheKey, isEligibleForRefund, handleTransaction, fetchFromDynamoDbAndHandle, and saveToDynamoDb as required
}
```

This design provides a high-level structure for the Java service. Developers can make critical implementation decisions based on the provided considerations. The solution is designed to handle high concurrency, distributed transactions, caching, error handling, and compliance with regulatory standards using Micronaut, Redis, Hystrix, and DynamoDB.

Developers should optimize for performance, resource management, and fault tolerance while considering factors like scalability, maintainability, and edge cases. They can choose between local in-memory cache and distributed cache solutions like Redis based on the application's specific requirements. Additionally, they should implement efficient transaction processing, robust error handling mechanisms, and design for long-term sustainability and ease of maintenance.']

