```

3. Create a Config class to handle dynamic configuration at runtime, allowing switching between log levels and output destinations without recompiling.

```c
struct Config {
    std::string output_destination;
    LogLevel min_log_level;
    // Other configuration options as needed (e.g., buffer sizes, rate limits)
};
```

4. Implement a Factory pattern to create Logger objects based on the provided configuration.

```c
class LoggerFactory {
public:
    static std::unique_ptr<Logger> create(const Config& config) {
        if (config.output_destination == "console")
            return std::make_unique<ConsoleLogger>();
        // Handle other output destinations similarly
        throw std::runtime_error("Unsupported output destination");
    }
};
```

5. Implement a LoggerManager class to handle multiple concurrent clients efficiently, with robust error handling for unexpected conditions.

```c
class LoggerManager {
private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::unique_ptr<Logger>> loggers;

public:
    void initialize(const Config& config) {
        for (auto& logger : loggers)
            logger.second->init(config);
    }

    void log(const std::string& service, LogLevel level, const std::string& message) {
        auto it = loggers.find(service);
        if (it == loggers.end())
            throw std::runtime_error("Unknown service");
        it->second->log(level, message);
    }
};
```

6. Implement the necessary support for structured logging in JSON format and advanced filtering capabilities based on log attributes.

7. Balance the trade-off between verbosity and performance by optimizing filtering mechanisms to avoid significant overhead during peak times.

